columns.prompt:
You are helping build training data for an AI assistant that supports Oregon Metro staff — a regional government agency serving the greater Portland area.

Read the GitHub {{ item_type }} below and write ONE realistic request from a Metro staff member (planner, GIS analyst, IT admin, or permit coordinator) that could have prompted it. Use first-person imperative phrasing ("Help me understand...", "Draft a PR that...", "Investigate why..."). Preserve concrete technical details (field names, file paths, tracebacks, layer names) from the thread. Keep it 1-3 sentences. Output ONLY the user request, no preamble.

--- INPUT ---
Repo: {{ repo }}
Type: {{ item_type }}
Title: {{ title }}
Body:
{{ body }}

First comments:
{{ comments }}

You are generating one training row for an Oregon Metro AI assistant. Metro is a regional government serving the greater Portland area. Its work spans GIS/land data, parks, waste management, transit planning, permitting, and public engagement.

Given the real GitHub thread and a synthesized Metro staff request, produce a grounded, helpful response.

Source thread:
- Repo: {{ repo }}
- Type: {{ item_type }}
- Title: {{ title }}
- URL: {{ url }}
- State: {{ state }}
- Labels: {{ labels }}
- Body: {{ body }}
- First comments: {{ comments }}

Staff request:
{{ User }}

Rules:
- `response`: 100-250 words of Markdown grounded in the thread. If closed/resolved, follow `issue_fix_plan`: brief diagnosis, numbered fix steps, short repro. If a PR, follow `explain_pr`: what changed, why, which files/symbols. If open/unresolved, answer honestly and ask for missing info.
- Always note if the topic touches PII, public records, or citizen-facing data — Metro must comply with Oregon public records law.
- Cite the source URL at least once inline as `[source: {{ url }}]`.
- Name at least one concrete symbol (function, field, layer, flag, or file path) from the thread when available.
- Include a short ```bash or ```python code block ONLY if the thread itself contains that code or command.
- `followups`: 0-4 follow-up questions when info is missing. Empty list if complete.
- `cites`: URLs/file paths actually used. Always include `{{ url }}`.
- `task`: one of `explain_pr`, `issue_fix_plan`, `issue_solution`, `discussion_qa`.
- `confidence`: `high` / `medium` / `low`.
